---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
// Mirrors turf's docs/telemetry.md (github.com/turfbuild/turf), field for field. A schema
// change lands in that doc first, then here; see "Content provenance" in CLAUDE.md.
const title = 'Turf — Telemetry: what it sends and how to turn it off';
const description =
  'Every field the Turf engine sends as anonymous usage telemetry, what it never sends, where the events go, and how to turn it off with one command.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="subhero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Telemetry</p>
      <h1>What Turf sends, and how to turn it off.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Turf™ sends anonymous usage telemetry to Turf LLC by default: the version, the
        platform, counts of resources, changes and runs, and an anonymous installation id. It never
        sends resource contents, credentials, state, variable values or names.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Turn it off</h2>
      <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix fields">
        <thead><tr><th>Where Turf runs</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>The Homebrew service</th><td class="note-cell"><code>turf telemetry off</code>, then
            <code>brew services restart turf</code>. Or put <code>TURF_TELEMETRY=off</code> or
            <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> in <code>~/.homebrew/services/turf.env</code> and restart the
            service. With Homebrew's analytics off (<code>brew analytics off</code> or
            <code>HOMEBREW_NO_ANALYTICS</code>), the service starts with telemetry off.</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>turf-mcp-server engine run</code> in a terminal</th><td class="note-cell"><code>turf telemetry off</code>
            before you start it, or <code>TURF_TELEMETRY=off</code> or <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> in
            that terminal.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>A container or CI</th><td class="note-cell"><code>TURF_TELEMETRY=off</code> or
            <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> in the engine's environment, or
            <code>turf-engine --telemetry=off</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>To install with telemetry off from the start, run the installer with
          <code>--no-telemetry</code>
          (<code>curl -fsSL https://get.turf.build/install.sh | bash -s -- --no-telemetry</code>), or with
          <code>TURF_TELEMETRY=off</code> or <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> in your shell. It runs
          <code>turf telemetry off</code> before the engine starts, so the setting covers the Homebrew
          service too.</p>
        <p><code>turf telemetry off</code> writes the setting to <code>$TURF_HOME/telemetry.json</code>
          (<code>~/.turf</code> by default). <code>turf-mcp-server engine run</code>, which the Homebrew
          service runs, reads it each time it starts the engine, so a change takes effect at the next
          start. A setting that is not valid JSON, or whose <code>enabled</code> is not
          <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>, starts the engine with telemetry off until
          <code>turf telemetry on</code> or <code>off</code> rewrites it. A <code>turf-engine</code> you
          run yourself, in a container or CI, reads no file: use the variables or the flag.</p>
        <p><code>TURF_TELEMETRY</code> takes precedence over <code>DO_NOT_TRACK</code>, and both take
          precedence over the setting. Setting either in your shell does not reach the Homebrew
          service, whose environment is <code>turf.env</code>. That file is
          <code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/homebrew/services/turf.env</code> if you set
          <code>XDG_CONFIG_HOME</code>, and Homebrew skips it if it is group- or world-writable.</p>
        <p><code>turf telemetry status</code> shows the setting and your installation id.
          <code>turf-mcp-server engine status</code> shows whether the running engine sends.
          <code>TURF_TELEMETRY_DEBUG=1</code> in the engine's environment writes each event to the
          engine's log instead of sending it, so you can read exactly what would leave.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What it sends</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The Turf engine sends it: <code>turf-engine</code>, which the <code>turf</code> Homebrew
          service runs. The <code>turf</code> CLI and <code>turf-mcp-server</code> send none of their
          own. The components Turf is built on send nothing either: restate-server's telemetry and the
          embedded agent runtime's are always off.</p>
        <p>There are three events. Each one is a single JSON <code>POST</code> to
          <code>https://telemetry.turf.build/v1/events</code>, whose only headers beyond the HTTP basics
          are <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> and
          <code>User-Agent: turf-engine/&lt;version&gt; (&lt;platform&gt;; &lt;arch&gt;)</code>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix fields">
        <caption>On every event</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th><code>schema</code></th><td class="note-cell">The event schema's version, <code>1</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>event</code></th><td class="note-cell"><code>engine.start</code>, <code>engine.daily</code> or <code>convergence</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>installation_id</code></th><td class="note-cell">A random UUID generated for your
            installation, stored in <code>telemetry.json</code>. It identifies an installation, not a
            person. An engine with no setting, such as one in a container or CI, sends none unless
            <code>TURF_INSTALLATION_ID</code> gives it one.</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>version</code></th><td class="note-cell">Turf's version</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>platform</code>, <code>arch</code></th><td class="note-cell">The operating system
            and CPU architecture, such as <code>darwin</code> and <code>arm64</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>deployment</code></th><td class="note-cell"><code>local</code> (the per-user
            engine), <code>container</code> or <code>standalone</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>install_method</code></th><td class="note-cell"><code>homebrew</code>, <code>archive</code> or <code>container</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>ci</code></th><td class="note-cell">Whether the engine runs under a CI system</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix fields">
        <caption><code>engine.start</code>, once each time the engine starts</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th><code>restate_version</code></th><td class="note-cell">The restate-server version the engine runs on</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>scopes</code>, <code>mise</code>, <code>plugin_cache</code>, <code>request_identity</code></th><td class="note-cell">Whether each engine feature is on</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix fields">
        <caption><code>engine.daily</code>, every 24 hours the engine has been running, idle or not</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th><code>uptime_days</code></th><td class="note-cell">Days since the engine started</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>convergences_24h</code>, <code>converged_24h</code>, <code>failed_24h</code></th><td class="note-cell">Runs in the last day: all, finished, failed</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>workspaces_24h</code>, <code>workspaces</code></th><td class="note-cell">How many
            workspaces ran in the last day, and since the engine started</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>managed_resources</code>, <code>managed_bucket</code></th><td class="note-cell">Managed
            resources across those workspaces, exact and bucketed</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <div class="matrix-wrap"><table class="matrix fields">
        <caption><code>convergence</code>, once per run (<code>turf up</code>, a destroy, a refresh, an import, an action invoke)</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th><code>mode</code></th><td class="note-cell"><code>up</code>, <code>destroy</code>,
            <code>refresh</code>, <code>import</code>, <code>invoke</code>, or <code>other</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>outcome</code></th><td class="note-cell"><code>converged</code>,
            <code>stalled</code>, <code>exhausted</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>declined</code>,
            <code>stale</code>, <code>cancelled</code>, <code>error</code>, or <code>other</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>failure</code></th><td class="note-cell">For a failed run: <code>plan</code>,
            <code>apply</code>, <code>checks</code>, <code>refused</code> or <code>engine</code>; else
            <code>none</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>refused</code></th><td class="note-cell">For a run refused over a Terraform®
            construct Turf does not run, the construct's name (such as <code>provisioners</code>).
            Always one of a fixed list, never your configuration's text.</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>rounds</code>, <code>deferred</code></th><td class="note-cell">How many
            plan-and-apply rounds the run took, and the changes it deferred between them</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>auto_approve</code>, <code>approvals_asked</code>, <code>approvals_auto</code>, <code>declined</code></th><td class="note-cell">How plans were approved</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>creates</code>, <code>updates</code>, <code>replaces</code>, <code>deletes</code>,
            <code>reads</code>, <code>noops</code>, <code>taints</code>, <code>forgets</code>,
            <code>imports</code>, <code>moves</code>, <code>drifted</code></th><td class="note-cell">How
            many planned changes of each kind, summed over the run's rounds</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>applied</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>cancelled</code></th><td class="note-cell">How
            many changes applied, failed or were cancelled</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>actions</code>, <code>actions_failed</code>, <code>turf_actions</code></th><td class="note-cell">How
            many actions ran, failed, and were Turf's own</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>providers</code>, <code>modules</code></th><td class="note-cell">The most providers
            and module instances any round's plan used. Never which.</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>managed_resources</code>, <code>data_sources</code></th><td class="note-cell">How
            many the workspace holds afterwards</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>backend</code></th><td class="note-cell">The state backend's type, such as
            <code>local</code> or <code>s3</code>, one of the backends built into OpenTofu™; any other is
            <code>other</code></td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>duration_s</code>, <code>plan_s</code>, <code>apply_s</code>, <code>wait_s</code></th><td class="note-cell">Seconds
            the run took, spent planning, applying, and waiting for approval</td></tr>
          <tr><th><code>changes_bucket</code>, <code>managed_bucket</code>, <code>duration_bucket</code></th><td class="note-cell">The
            same numbers in ranges, such as <code>6-25</code> or <code>1-5m</code>;
            <code>duration_bucket</code> is <code>unknown</code> for a run that failed before its plan
            was ready</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What it never sends</h2>
      <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>Resource addresses, names or ids, and the values or contents of anything you manage.</li>
        <li>Workspace names, state, plans, configuration files or paths, and module sources.</li>
        <li>Provider names or registry hosts.</li>
        <li>Variable or output values, credentials, and backend configuration.</li>
        <li>Error messages, diagnostics, prompts and model output.</li>
        <li>Hostnames, user names and cloud account ids.</li>
      </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Where it goes</h2>
      <div class="prose">
      <p>Events go to <code>telemetry.turf.build</code>, a hostname Turf LLC controls, which Scarf
        operates for Turf LLC. Like any HTTP request, an event arrives from your network's IP address.
        Scarf uses the address to estimate the organization and the approximate location the event
        comes from, then discards it (<a href="https://docs.scarf.sh/company-matching/">how</a>). For
        the address, Scarf is Turf LLC's data processor under a data processing agreement, and acts
        only on Turf LLC's instructions. Scarf's terms of use also give Scarf a license to use, and to
        share, the rest of what it receives for its own purposes, such as running and improving its services
        (<a href="https://about.scarf.sh/terms-of-use/#4-data">section 4</a>). Turf LLC uses events to
        understand how Turf is used, to see which installations are active, and to size commercial
        licenses.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Page-local: every table here is name | meaning, and a shared first-column width keeps the
     five lined up as one reference instead of each sizing to its longest field list. */
  table.fields { table-layout: fixed; }
  table.fields th:first-child { width: 38%; }
  /* At phone width a two-column table would scroll sideways inside .matrix-wrap and hide the
     meanings, so each field stacks over its meaning instead. */
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    table.fields { min-width: 0; table-layout: auto; }
    table.fields thead { display: none; }
    table.fields tr, table.fields th, table.fields td { display: block; }
    table.fields th:first-child { width: auto; }
    table.fields tbody td { border-top: 0; padding-top: 0; }
  }
</style>
